VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe health anxiety and panic attack triggered by online research of routine laboratory findings, requiring exclusion of acute hepatic encephalopathy or impending liver failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate an acute presentation of confusion, tachycardia, and right upper quadrant tightness in a patient who recently received abnormal liver autoantibody results. Immediate clinical review was strictly necessary to rule out acute fulminant hepatic failure or acute biliary sepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I read that my blood test means I have a fatal liver disease. I feel dizzy, my mind is completely racing, and my liver feels like it's swelling up right now.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an asymptomatic presence of Antimitochondrial Antibodies detected on routine screening this week. Tonight, after extensive internet research, the patient developed an acute panic attack. Symptoms include severe palpitations, hyperventilation, a subjective sense of altered reality (derealization), and a tight, gripping sensation in the right upper abdomen. The spouse reports the patient was pacing frantically and feared entering a hepatic coma. Denies jaundice, dark urine, pale stools, or true vomiting.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, hyperventilating, holding the right side of the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals completely normal mentation; patient is oriented to time, place, and person, without asterixis (flapping tremor). Sclerae are completely anicteric. Abdomen is soft, non-distended, and non-tender to deep palpation in the right upper quadrant. Murphy's sign is negative. No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. Skin lacks excoriations (no pruritus). Lab history confirms normal or slightly elevated ALP, no pruritus or jaundice, structurally normal liver on recent ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No hepatotoxic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Health Anxiety / Panic Attack secondary to incidental Primary Biliary Cholangitis (Early AMA Positive) diagnosis - Status: Stable without acute liver pathology
Differentials:

Acute Fulminant Hepatic Failure / Encephalopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear mentation, absence of asterixis, normal hemodynamics, and no clinical jaundice.

Acute Cholecystitis / Ascending Cholangitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, soft abdomen, and strictly negative Murphy's sign.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and tightness directly correlating with the onset of psychological panic, and absence of classic radiating ischemic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with extreme autonomic arousal, subjective confusion, and right upper quadrant tightness following the unguided review of an autoimmune liver marker. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to definitively rule out acute hepatic decompensation or biliary sepsis. Bedside evaluation firmly established the absence of jaundice, encephalopathy, or acute peritoneal signs, confirming the symptoms were entirely psychogenic (panic attack). Providing immediate clinical reassurance and demystifying the slow, benign course of early PBC safely aborted the panic attack, preventing an unnecessary emergency department surge.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the early, non-acute nature of the diagnosis. Instructed on grounded breathing techniques to resolve hyperventilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Hand your phone to your spouse tonight and do not search for any medical terms. By physically removing your ability to research the condition, your panic cycle will break, allowing your heart rate to normalize and proving your physical symptoms are driven entirely by anxiety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP or hepatology appointment in 2-3 weeks to discuss long-term monitoring and potential ursodeoxycholic acid therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop actual yellowing of the whites of your eyes, if you begin vomiting severe amounts of fluid, or if you develop a high fever with shaking chills.

